  "last_message": "This exact change was explicitly rejected by an existing decision: ADR 0001 \"Keep sessions in the database\" (`docs/adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md`). It records that larder tried in-process session memory in 2023, which logged everyone out on every deploy and whenever a request hit a different server behind the load balancer \u2014 that's why sessions moved to Postgres. A per-process dict in `session_store.py` would reintroduce exactly that bug.\n\nI'd rather not make this change silently. Options:\n\n1. Don't make the change, and explain why (point you to the ADR).\n2. If you still want speed improvements, something like an in-process cache that's still backed by Postgres (write-through, not a replacement) wouldn't violate the ADR's constraints \u2014 but that's a different design than \"use it instead of PostgresSessions.\"\n\nDo you want me to skip this change, or did you have something in mind that addresses the deploy/multi-server logout problem (e.g., a cache in front of Postgres rather than a replacement)?",
